  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThere is currently moist, southwesterly flow aloft over \nsouthern Idaho with light showers currently, mainly over our \nthe Central Mountains, western areas and the Island Park/Driggs\nareas. Currently, there is an upper level low centered just off\nthe coasts of Washington and Oregon as part of an overall \ntrough that extends much farther south to off the coast of \nCalifornia, where there is ample sub-tropical moisture \navailable. This low/trough is bringing our current moisture and \nwill drive the weather/precipitation today and Sunday, \nespecially as this low/trough moves over our area late today \ninto Sunday. \n\nPrecipitation for lower elevations today has dropped for the\nSnake Plain, southeastern areas (Bear Lake Region), and northern\nareas (Stanley and Challis) where a T to 0.04 is expected. The \nSun Valley Region and Island Park region will receive around\n0.05 to 0.15 today. This precipitation will likely have some \nimpacts on Halloween activities. However, the bulk of the \nprecipitation is still arriving later this evening through \nSunday and lingering into Monday morning. \n\nBy late today into Sunday the low off the Washington coast \nbegins to move and makes it way inland aided by an deepening \ntrough, which will really picks up more Pacific moisture and \ntransports it over southeastern Idaho. This more organized round\nof precipitation will begin in the Central Mountains/Wood River\nValley/eastern Magic Valley as early as 6 PM today and then \nspread eastward throughout the night. The NBM model is still \nshowing about an 90 to 100 percent chance of the entire CWA \nreceiving at least 0.25 inch of precipitation with this system. \nPrecipitation amounts are up about 0.15 from yesterday's model\nround during day and down about 0.25 from last nights model \nrun. It still seems likely that many will see 0.20 to 0.50 \ninches in the eastern Magic Valley and Challis area, 0.50 to \n0.80 inches in the Snake River Plain and lower elevations of the\nEastern Highlands and Central Mountains. For higher elevations \n(above 8000 feet) precipitation will be pushing 0.80 to 1.25 \ninches, especially for Sun Valley Region, Big Lost \nHighlands/Copper Basin, Lost River Range, and the Montana Divide\nas well as the Southern areas along the Utah border including \nthe Wasatch mountains. Temperatures will still be slightly above\nnormal today with highs in the 40s and 50s, but by Sunday our \nhighs will drop into the mid and upper 30s and 40s. Snow impacts\nare likely at Galena Summit today into Sunday morning with the \nforecast still showing 3-7\" of snow there. A winter weather \nadvisory is still in effect for the Sun Valley Region and the \nBig Lost Highlands above 7000 feet for this evening through \nSunday. Elsewhere, the bulk of our snow is expected to remain \nabove most of the rest of our mountain passes, but that could \nchange easily with temperature changes of just a few degrees. \nEven though snow levels will start high, convection can lower \nsnow levels, which could drop snow levels to pass levels \nespecially for areas like Emigration and Raynolds pass. \n\nA few showers linger on Monday morning, but we will be drying \nout from west to east by that point and high pressure builds in \non Monday. The breakdown of this upper level ridge is still in \nquestion. There remain four different clusters of solutions in \nthis medium range period. It is about a 60-40 split that the \nridge will remain on Wednesday, then flatter, zonal flow or weak\ntroughing developing Thursday. Zonal flow makes timing of \nshortwave energy more difficult, so there is quite a bit of \nuncertainty in the forecast in the long-term.",
